Third-party data and software
-----------------------------

OpenStreetMap
  Road network, buildings, districts and points of interest are derived from
  OpenStreetMap data, (c) OpenStreetMap contributors, available under the
  Open Database License (ODbL) 1.0: https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright
  Extracts are obtained from Geofabrik GmbH (https://download.geofabrik.de/).
  Derived databases (for example data/demo/*.net.xml and data/jeddah/*) remain
  subject to the ODbL; attribution "(c) OpenStreetMap contributors" must be kept.

Eclipse SUMO
  This project uses Eclipse SUMO (Simulation of Urban MObility), (c) German
  Aerospace Center (DLR) and others, under the Eclipse Public License 2.0
  (EPL-2.0): https://eclipse.dev/sumo/ . SUMO is installed as a dependency
  (PyPI wheel "eclipse-sumo"), not vendored.

GASTAT open data
  Population and labour statistics come from the General Authority for
  Statistics of the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia (GASTAT), https://www.stats.gov.sa/ ,
  used under its open-data terms of use, with attribution to GASTAT.

Esri World Imagery
  The lane audit (sim/network/lane_audit.py) reads lane counts from Esri World Imagery tiles
  (https://server.arcgisonline.com/ArcGIS/rest/services/World_Imagery/MapServer). Imagery: Esri,
  Maxar, Earthstar Geographics, and the GIS User Community. The audit is blocked on accuracy, so no
  imagery-derived lane count is applied to the network, and no imagery is redistributed in this
  repository (crops stay local under data/imagery/ and docs/img/lane-audit/, both gitignored).

Basemap tiles in the viewer
  The web viewer loads its background map at runtime from OpenFreeMap
  (https://openfreemap.org/, style "dark"): OpenFreeMap (c) OpenMapTiles
  (https://openmaptiles.org/), data (c) OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL). No
  tiles are redistributed; the viewer shows the attribution on the map. The films
  use an offline basemap built from the Geofabrik extract (OpenStreetMap, ODbL).

Images and films
  Screenshots, stills and GIF previews under docs/img/ (and films rendered into
  movies/) depict OpenStreetMap-derived data and are Produced Works under the
  ODbL: keep the attribution "(c) OpenStreetMap contributors" when reusing them.
  Title cards are set with fonts installed on the rendering machine; no font
  files are redistributed in this repository.

Third-party software (dependencies, not vendored)
  Python (installed by uv from uv.lock): fastapi, starlette, uvicorn, pydantic,
  typer, numpy, pandas, pyarrow, duckdb, shapely, pyproj, geopandas, pyogrio,
  matplotlib, pillow, pyyaml, psutil, adhanpy and their dependencies, under
  permissive licences (MIT, BSD-2/3-Clause, Apache-2.0, ISC, PSF-2.0, MIT-CMU,
  matplotlib licence) except certifi (MPL-2.0, unmodified) and SUMO's sumolib,
  traci and eclipse-sumo (EPL-2.0 OR GPL-2.0-or-later).
  Web viewer (installed by npm from web/package-lock.json): React (MIT),
  deck.gl (MIT), MapLibre GL JS (BSD-3-Clause), Vite and TypeScript (dev only)
  and their dependencies, under MIT, ISC, BSD-2/3-Clause, Apache-2.0 and 0BSD
  licences; jszip is used under its MIT option and pako under MIT AND Zlib.
  The full per-package inventory is in docs/release-audit.md.

Kokoro-82M (film narration)
  The voice-over of the films (scripts/movies/narrate.py) is synthesised offline with Kokoro-82M,
  (c) hexgrad, code and model weights under the Apache License 2.0:
  https://huggingface.co/hexgrad/Kokoro-82M (kokoro 0.9.4, model revision f3ff3571, voice
  af_heart). It runs in a separate, pinned environment (.venv-tts, scripts/movies/tts-requirements.txt)
  with misaki (Apache-2.0), PyTorch (BSD-3-Clause), spaCy and en_core_web_sm (MIT); none of it
  is vendored. The rendered audio is generated by this project; no cloud TTS service is used.

Chatterbox Multilingual and ClArTTS (Arabic film narration)
  The Arabic voice-over (movies/<film>.ar.mp4, scripts/movies/tts_ar.py) is synthesised offline
  with Chatterbox Multilingual, (c) Resemble AI, code and model weights under the MIT licence:
  https://huggingface.co/ResembleAI/chatterbox (chatterbox-tts 0.1.7, revision 5bb1f6ee). Its
  outputs carry Resemble's inaudible Perth watermark. The voice is cloned from one utterance of
  ClArTTS (ch_02_arabic_tts_dataset_417), MBZUAI, CC-BY-4.0:
  https://huggingface.co/datasets/MBZUAI/ClArTTS; Kulkarni et al., "ClArTTS: An Open-Source
  Classical Arabic Text-to-Speech Corpus", Interspeech 2023 (audio from a LibriVox recording). The
  clip is downloaded at setup and not redistributed. It runs in a separate, pinned environment
  (.venv-tts-ar, scripts/movies/tts-ar-requirements.txt) with PyTorch (BSD-3-Clause); the sample
  checks use Whisper large-v3-turbo (MIT) through mlx-whisper (MIT). None of it is vendored.

Synthetic demand
  All simulation results are produced from synthetic demand and are
  uncalibrated unless explicitly stated otherwise.
